WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:04.410
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.580
Today we are going to talk about the craft
of simulating rays of light to create beautiful

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.839
images, just like the ones you see here.

00:00:11.839 --> 00:00:16.680
And when I say simulating rays of light, I
mean not a few, but millions and millions

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.750
of light rays need to be computed, alongside
with how they get absorbed or scattered off

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.710
of our objects in a virtual scene.

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:29.100
Initially, we start out with a really noisy
image, and as we add more rays, the image

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.660
gets clearer and clearer over time.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.620
The time it takes for these images to clean
up depends on the complexity of the geometry

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:41.730
and our material models, and one thing is
for sure: rendering materials that have multiple

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:43.610
layers is a nightmare.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:48.390
This paper introduces an amazing new multilayer
material model to address that.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:53.820
Here you see an example where we are able
to stack together transparent and translucent

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.460
layers to synthesize a really lifelike scratched
metal material with water droplets.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.600
Also, have a look at these gorgeous materials.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:07.640
And note that these are all virtual materials
that are simulated using physics and computer

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.890
graphics.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.890
Isn't this incredible?

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:15.409
However, some of you Fellow Scholars remember
that we talked about multilayered materials

00:01:15.409 --> 00:01:16.700
before.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.649
So what's new here?

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:23.460
This new method supports more advanced material
models that previous techniques were either

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.409
unable to simulate, or took too long to do
so.

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:29.770
But that's not all - have a look here.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.530
What you see is an equal-time comparison,
which means that if we run the new technique

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.740
against the older methods for the same amount
of time, it is easy to see that we will have

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.280
much less noise in our output image.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.069
This means that the images clear up quicker
and we can produce them in less time.

00:01:47.069 --> 00:01:52.539
It also supports my favorite, multiple importance
sampling, an aggressive noise-reduction technique

00:01:52.539 --> 00:01:57.719
by Eric Veach which is arguably one of the
greatest inventions ever in light transport

00:01:57.719 --> 00:01:58.749
research.

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:04.179
This ensures that for more difficult scenes,
the images clean up much, much faster and

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:07.530
has a beautiful and simple mathematical formulation.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:12.100
Super happy to see that it also earned him
a technical Oscar award a few years ago.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:16.580
If you are enjoying learning about light transport,
make sure to check out my course on this topic

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.630
at the Technical University of Vienna.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:23.850
I still teach this at the University for 20
Master students at a time and thought that

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:29.060
the teachings shouldn't only be available
for a lucky few people who can afford a college

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:30.060
education.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.850
Clearly, the teachings should be available
for everyone, so we recorded it and put it

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:38.430
online, and now everyone can watch it, free
of charge.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:43.351
I was quite stunned to see that more than
10 thousand people decided to start it, so

00:02:43.351 --> 00:02:45.950
make sure to give it a go if you're interested!

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:50.900
And just one more thing: as you are listening
to this episode, I am holding a talk at the

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.870
EU's Political Strategy Centre.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.180
And the objective of this talk is to inform
political decisionmakers about the state of

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.780
the art in AI so they can make more informed
decisions for us.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.700
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

